Two distinct failure regimes emerge from these counts.
Tier~1 conditions (accuracy ${>}$99\%) fail conservatively: all errors are false negatives (pass samples predicted as fail), with zero false positives across 339 fail-class samples.
Tier~2 conditions (accuracy ${<}$87\%) exhibit bidirectional errors dominated by false positives, where \texttt{AssertionError} (programs that produce incorrect output without raising distinctive exceptions) accounts for 60--100\% of FP errors.
Only 7 of 430 unique Tier~2 error samples overlap with Tier~1 errors, and zero samples are misclassified by all eight conditions, confirming that the two tiers fail on largely independent sample subsets (see also Section~\ref{sec:error_overlap}).
